  "contracts": {
    "def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth, Hausdorff, second-countable four-manifold without boundary, with smooth Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and smooth time-orientation field $N$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Supply an independent orientation $o$ of $M$ in the sense of [[def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart]]. The metric and time orientation do not select $o$. Write $\\mathrm{vol}_g$ for the oriented metric volume form of [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]]; in any positively oriented chart it has components $\\sqrt{|\\det(g_{ab})|}\\,dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-electrovacuum-cauchy-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$\\kappa:=\\frac{8\\pi G_N}{c^4}>0.$$\n\nThese are parameters of the mathematical system. For the SI interpretation, $c$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $G_N$ has units $\\mathrm{m^3\\,kg^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$, $\\mu_0$ has units $\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$, $\\kappa$ has units $\\mathrm{s^2\\,kg^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$, and $\\Lambda$ has units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$. In a length-coordinate chart $x^0=ct$, metric components are dimensionless and curvature components have units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$.\n\nFor a smooth real two-form $F\\in\\Omega^2(M)$, define",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-emg-charged-scalar-functional",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Here $\\kappa_0>0$ is the chosen circle normalization with $[\\kappa_0]=\\mathrm{Wb}$, $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ is the representation weight, and $\\lambda:=k/\\kappa_0$ has units $\\mathrm{Wb}^{-1}$. Because $g_{ij}g_{jk}=g_{ik}$ and $k$ is an integer, the maps $g_{ij}^{k}$ satisfy the scalar transition cocycle. A charged scalar is a family of smooth local functions $\\varphi_i$ satisfying this rule; all phase-lift calculations below are local, and no global phase lift is assumed. The constant $c>0$ is the length-to-time conversion parameter of [[def-emg-lorentzian-forms]], with units $\\mathrm m\\,\\mathrm s^{-1}$. Define $F|_{U_i}:=dA_i$; the transition law gives $dA_j=dA_i$, so $F$ is a global curvature two-form. In a global real-potential model on a trivial bundle, one may instead take a global $A$ and any real $\\lambda$; no charge quantization is imposed there.\n\nIn a local trivialization define the gauge-covariant derivative and its lower-index one-form by\n\n$$P_a:=(D\\varphi)_a:=\\partial_a\\varphi_i-i\\lambda A_{i,a}\\varphi_i.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In particular $\\star_g^2=-\\operatorname{id}$ on two-forms. For a two-form $F$, the component formula in a positively oriented chart is\n\n$$({\\star_gF})_{ab}=\\frac12(\\mathrm{vol}_g)_{abcd}F^{cd}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Since $A$ and $\\varphi$ are global smooth fields, $F=dA$, $\\pi=D\\varphi$, and $D\\pi$ are global smooth fields of the stated real or complex tensor types. The composition $V(|\\varphi|^2)$ is smooth and real because $|\\varphi|^2(M)\\subset\\mathcal U$ and $V$ is smooth and real-valued. The kinetic contraction $g^{ab}\\overline{\\pi_a}\\pi_b$ is real: complex conjugation followed by exchanging the dummy indices $a,b$ leaves it unchanged because $g^{ab}=g^{ba}$. Thus $L_\\varphi$ is a global smooth real scalar, and its product with $\\mathrm{vol}_g$ is a real smooth four-form. On a compact set its integral is finite; on noncompact $M$ the Definition uses only finite integrals or compactly supported differences, so it assigns no divergent action value.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L2",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "The tensor $\\operatorname{Re}(\\overline{\\pi_a}\\pi_b)$ is symmetric because interchanging $a,b$ conjugates the scalar and real part is unchanged. The Maxwell bilinear $F_{ac}F_b{}^c$ is also symmetric: after lowering the contracted index it is $g^{cd}F_{ac}F_{bd}$, which is unchanged under $a\\leftrightarrow b$ by symmetry of $g^{cd}$ and commutativity of scalar multiplication. Hence $T^\\varphi$, $T^{\\mathrm{EM}}$, and $T$ are smooth symmetric covariant two-tensors, while $j$ is a smooth vector field. The equations therefore compare tensors of the displayed types, with no variation or empirical assertion added to the definition.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "In length coordinates, $[D_a\\varphi]=[\\pi_a]=\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$, so both terms of $L_\\varphi$ have units $\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$ and $[S_\\varphi]=(\\mathrm{J\\,s\\,m^{-2}})(\\mathrm{m^{-2}})(\\mathrm{m^4})=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. The scalar stress has units $[K_\\varphi][\\pi]^2=(\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-1}})(\\mathrm{m^{-2}})=\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. The electromagnetic stress has units $[F]^2/[\\mu_0]=(\\mathrm{Wb^2\\,m^{-4}})/(\\mathrm{H\\,m^{-1}})=\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$, using $\\mathrm{H}=\\mathrm{Wb\\,A^{-1}}$ and $\\mathrm{Wb}=\\mathrm{J\\,A^{-1}}$. Also $[j^a]=(\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-1}})(\\mathrm{Wb^{-1}})(\\mathrm{m^{-1}})=\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$. Finally $[\\kappa]=8\\pi[G_N]/[c]^4=\\mathrm{s^2\\,kg^{-1}\\,m^{-1}}$, hence $[\\kappa T_{ab}]=\\mathrm{m^{-2}}$, matching curvature and $\\Lambda$. These are dimensional identities under the assigned SI bookkeeping.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No empty-domain, empty-state, or empty-boundary claim is made."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Zero fields are permitted and all tensors remain algebraically defined; step 1.2 uses no inverse of F."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
