    "lem-emg-temporal-scalar-positive-symmetric-reduction": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The associated **classical charged-scalar Einstein–Maxwell system** is",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-emg-lorentzian-forms",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a smooth, Hausdorff, second-countable four-manifold without boundary, with smooth Lorentzian metric $g$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ and smooth time-orientation field $N$, as in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]]. Supply an independent orientation $o$ of $M$ in the sense of [[def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart]]. The metric and time orientation do not select $o$. Write $\\mathrm{vol}_g$ for the oriented metric volume form of [[def-gr-metric-volume-form-and-current-flux]]; in any positively oriented chart it has components $\\sqrt{|\\det(g_{ab})|}\\,dx^0\\wedge dx^1\\wedge dx^2\\wedge dx^3$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Coefficients.** A **periodic symmetric hyperbolic coefficient family** on\n$O$ consists of smooth maps\n$$A^\\mu:I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O\\to\\operatorname{Sym}_m(\\mathbb R)\\quad(\\mu=0,1,2,3),\\qquad B:I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^m,$$\nsuch that $A^0(t,x,u)$ is positive definite for every\n$(t,x,u)\\in I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$; here\n$\\operatorname{Sym}_m(\\mathbb R)$ is the real vector space of symmetric\n$m\\times m$ matrices, identified with symmetric endomorphisms of $\\mathbb R^m$\nthrough the standard coordinate inner product, and positive definiteness means\n$z^TA^0(t,x,u)z>0$ for every nonzero $z\\in\\mathbb R^m$\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]).",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Expanding the divergence equation and retaining only derivatives of the independent $\\pi$ variables gives $g^{00}\\partial_s\\pi_0+g^{0i}\\partial_s\\pi_i+g^{i0}\\partial_i\\pi_0+g^{ij}\\partial_i\\pi_j$. Substitute $\\partial_s\\pi_i=\\partial_i\\pi_0-i\\lambda A_i\\pi_0-i\\lambda F_{0i}\\varphi$ from the compatibility equations. Since $g^{i0}=g^{0i}$, the derivative terms become $g^{00}\\partial_s\\pi_0+2g^{0i}\\partial_i\\pi_0+g^{ij}\\partial_i\\pi_j$; the substituted source terms contain no derivatives of $\\pi$. Multiplying this row by $-1$ gives the top row of the stated principal system.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For each $k$, multiplying the $i$th compatibility equation by $g^{ki}$ gives time coefficient $g^{ki}\\partial_s\\pi_i$ and spatial coefficient $-g^{ki}\\partial_k\\pi_0$. Thus the complete $\\pi$-block has exactly the displayed $A^0,A^k$. The time block is positive because $-g^{00}>0$ and $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite. Each spatial block is symmetric because $g^{ij}=g^{ji}$, including the off-diagonal entries $-g^{kj}$ and $-g^{jk}$. The same calculation applies separately to the real and imaginary parts.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The equations $\\partial_s\\varphi=\\pi_0$ and $\\partial_sA_i=F_{0i}$ contain no spatial derivatives of their evolved variables, so they append identity time blocks and zero spatial blocks. The connection terms in $D_a\\pi^a$, the gauge terms, the potential term, and derivatives of the prescribed metric coefficients are zero-order terms once the first metric derivatives are included in the coupled first-order state. Smoothness of inversion on the stated metric domain and smoothness of $V$ on the attained open range make every coefficient map smooth there. By [L1] this verifies the coefficient definition, without asserting existence of a solution to the coupled system.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
