    "lem-emg-charged-scalar-subsidiary-chain": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The associated **classical charged-scalar Einstein–Maxwell system** is",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-emg-temporal-scalar-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "where $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ and $D_a\\pi^a:=g^{ab}D_a\\pi_b$. The temporal-gauge scalar first-order system is",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "lem-emg-charged-scalar-variation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then the scalar-field variation is $\\delta_\\varphi S_\\varphi=\\frac{K_\\varphi}{c}\\operatorname{Re}\\int_M\\overline{\\delta\\varphi}\\,E_\\varphi\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g$; stationarity in every compactly supported complex scalar direction is equivalent to $E_\\varphi=0$. The connection and inverse-metric variations are",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-emg-maxwell-stress-divergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The six evolution equations for $(e,b)$ have principal part\n$$n(e)-\\operatorname{curl}_h b=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b),\\qquad n(b)+\\operatorname{curl}_h e=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b).$$\nFor a spatial covector $\\xi$, let $C(\\xi)v:=\\xi^\\sharp\\times v$ in the oriented orthonormal frame. Set $\\theta^0:=\\alpha ds=-g(n,\\cdot)$ and decompose any spacetime covector as $\\zeta=\\zeta(n)\\theta^0+\\zeta_\\perp$, where $\\zeta_\\perp(n)=0$ and $|\\zeta_\\perp|_h$ is its spatial norm. The spatial principal block is the linear map on $\\mathbb R^3\\oplus\\mathbb R^3$ defined by $S(\\xi)(u,v)=(-C(\\xi)v,C(\\xi)u)$. It is real symmetric, has eigenvalues $0,0,|\\xi|,|\\xi|,-|\\xi|,-|\\xi|$, and satisfies $\\|S(\\xi)\\|=|\\xi|$. Therefore the principal symbol $P(\\zeta)=\\zeta(n)I_6+S(\\zeta_\\perp)$ is positive definite for every covector with $\\zeta(n)>|\\zeta_\\perp|_h$. In the adapted coordinates its time matrix is $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$, and every coordinate spatial matrix is symmetric. Derivatives of the frame and metric enter only as smooth lower-order coefficients depending on $(g,g^{-1},\\partial g,e,b)$; in a first-order metric state they are zero-order terms. The remaining two Maxwell equations are the constraints $\\operatorname{div}_h e=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}_h b=0$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L6",
          "source": "lem-emg-coupled-reduced-einstein-evolution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer and let $U=(g_{ab},p_{ab},q_{abi},e_i,b_i)$ be the finite-dimensional first-order state on the $2\\pi$-periodic three-torus, with $p_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$ and $q_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$ for a fixed smooth background connection $\\bar\\nabla$. The component Sobolev spaces $H^s$ are those of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. Assume an open admissible state set $O$ on which $g$ is Lorentzian, $g^{00}<0$, the inverse spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite, the lapse is positive, and the chosen state variables and coefficients are smooth. Let $K\\Subset\\operatorname{int}K'\\Subset O$ be compact sets and $R\\ge0$. Assume the draft local symmetric-hyperbolic supplier [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]] with its stated integer-$s$, coefficient, compact-state, choice and norm hypotheses. Then the harmonic-coordinate reduced Einstein equation with source $T_{ab}=\\mu_0^{-1}(F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\tfrac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd})$, coupled to the six source-free Maxwell field-strength evolution equations, is a smooth quasilinear symmetric-hyperbolic system. For every initial state $U_0\\in H^s$ taking values in $K$ and satisfying $\\|U_0\\|_{H^s}\\le R$, its reduced system has a local solution with the regularity and compact-state bounds of [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]]. No Einstein or Maxwell constraint is assumed in this reduced-system statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L6",
          "source": "lem-gr-reduced-einstein-principal-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b) [first-order reduction]** Fix constants $\\kappa>0$, $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ and a prescribed smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor field $T$ on the chart domain. Fix a chart $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ with\n$x^0=t$. Introduce the metric variables\n$$u:=\\bigl(g_{ab},\\ p_{ab}:=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab},\\ q_{abi}:=\\bar\\nabla_ig_{ab}\\bigr)$$\n(symmetry in $a,b$ understood) and the relation variables $\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}-p_{ab}=0$ and $\\bar\\nabla_0q_{abi}-\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}=\\mathcal C_{abi}$, where $\\mathcal C_{abi}=-\\bar R^c{}_{a0i}g_{cb}-\\bar R^c{}_{b0i}g_{ac}$. Second covariant derivatives include their derivative-slot connection. These known background-curvature terms are zeroth order in the first-order state and vanish for the flat coordinate background. Then the reduced Einstein equation can be solved for the second time derivatives in the form\n$$\\bar\\nabla_0p_{ab}=-(g^{00})^{-1}\\Bigl[2g^{0i}\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}+g^{ij}\\bar\\nabla_iq_{abj}+R_{ab}(x,u,T;\\kappa,\\Lambda,\\bar g)\\Bigr],$$\nwhere the source is smooth in the first-order metric state and the prescribed matter data, with no derivatives of $T$, and includes $2\\kappa(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12(g^{cd}T_{cd})g_{ab})+2\\Lambda g_{ab}$. The resulting first-order system for $u$ is symmetric hyperbolic on every\nregion where $g^{00}<0$ and the spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite:\nthe coefficient of $\\bar\\nabla_0u$ is the block diagonal\n$\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ (on the three slots\n$g,p,q$), which is positive definite there, and the coefficients of the spatial\nderivatives are symmetric. More generally, in a coupled reduction one may substitute a specified smooth algebraic stress map $T_{ab}=\\mathcal T_{ab}(x,u,z)$, symmetric in $a,b$, where $z$ is a specified additional first-order matter state ranging in an open Euclidean set. This keeps the same metric principal matrices; symmetric hyperbolicity of the combined metric-matter system requires a separate check of the matter equations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "source": "lem-emg-lorentz-hodge-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In particular $\\star_g^2=-\\operatorname{id}$ on two-forms. For a two-form $F$, the component formula in a positively oriented chart is\n\n$$({\\star_gF})_{ab}=\\frac12(\\mathrm{vol}_g)_{abcd}F^{cd}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L8",
          "source": "thm-gr-density-divergence-and-finite-chain-flux",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(a) Exterior derivative of the current form.** As smooth $4$-forms on $M$,",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L9",
          "source": "def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Gauge vector.** The **harmonic-gauge vector field** of the pair\n$(g,\\bar g)$ is the smooth vector field\n$$H^a:=g^{bc}\\bigl(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\\bigr) .$$\nThe difference of the connection coefficients is a $(1,2)$-tensor, so $H$ is a\nwell-defined smooth vector field independent of the chart used to express the\nsymbols ([[def-gr-tensor-fields-and-components]]).\n\n**(b) Reduced Einstein equation.** The **reduced Einstein equation** for the\npair $(g,T)$ with respect to $\\bar g$ is the second-order quasilinear system\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}-\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\kappa\\bigl(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12Tg_{ab}\\bigr)+\\Lambda g_{ab},$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}$ is the Ricci tensor of $g$\n([[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]),\n$H_b=g_{bc}H^c$ and $\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\tfrac12(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a)$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L10",
          "source": "lem-gr-einstein-residual-and-subsidiary-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(a)** On every open set where $(g,T)$ solves the reduced Einstein equation, the identity\n$$E_{ab}=\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a-g_{ab}\\nabla_cH^c\\bigr)$$\nholds on that set; and",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L11",
          "source": "thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(c) Normal projections.** Define $(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(Y)=h^{ij}(D_{e_i}K)(e_j,Y)$, $\\tau=\\operatorname{tr}_hK=h^{ij}K_{ij}$ and $|K|_h^2=h^{ik}h^{jl}K_{ij}K_{kl}$. Then\n$$G(n,n)=\\tfrac12(S_h+\\tau^2-|K|_h^2),\\qquad G(n,Y)=(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(Y)-D_Y\\tau.$$",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L12",
          "source": "thm-divergence-theorem-for-bounded-piecewise-c-one-domains",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. If Omega has the specified finite piecewise $C^1$ presentation and $F\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n)$, then $\\int_\\Omega\\operatorname{div}F=\\sum_j\\int_{S_j}F\\cdot\\nu_j\\,dS$, with faces counted once off E. All integrals are finite. In a specified finite gluing, the two fluxes on every shared face cancel.",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L12",
          "source": "thm-gronwall-integral-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $t_0\\le T$, let $u,a,b:[t_0,T]\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous, with $u,b\\ge0$, and suppose",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L13",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let integers $s\\ge m\\ge0$ satisfy $s-m>3/2$, and\nlet $u\\in H^s(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R^N)$ with the objects and notation of\n[[def-gr-periodic-torus-and-sobolev-spaces]]. Then:",
          "uses": [
            "6.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Differentiate $C_i$ in $s$ and group the compatibility equation from [L2]: $\\partial_sC_i=(\\partial_s\\pi_i-\\partial_i\\pi_0)+i\\lambda(\\partial_sA_i)\\varphi+i\\lambda A_i\\partial_s\\varphi=(-i\\lambda A_i\\pi_0-i\\lambda F_{0i}\\varphi)+i\\lambda F_{0i}\\varphi+i\\lambda A_i\\pi_0=0$. Thus $C_i=0$ on the initial slice implies $C_i=0$ throughout the local time region, and the definition of $\\pi_i$ in [L1] gives $\\pi_i=D_i\\varphi$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Let $R_{abi}:=q_{abi}-\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$. The first-order metric equations in [L6] include $\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}=p_{ab}$ and $\\bar\\nabla_0q_{abi}-\\bar\\nabla_i p_{ab}=\\mathcal C_{abi}$, where $\\mathcal C_{abi}=[\\bar\\nabla_0,\\bar\\nabla_i]g_{ab}$. Therefore $\\bar\\nabla_0R_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i p_{ab}+\\mathcal C_{abi}-\\bar\\nabla_0\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_i\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}+[\\bar\\nabla_0,\\bar\\nabla_i]g_{ab}-\\bar\\nabla_0\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}=0$. The initial metric jet gives $R_{abi}=0$, so the first-order spatial metric variables remain the actual derivatives of $g$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L6",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The $(0,i,j)$ evolution component of $dF=0$ is $\\partial_sF_{ij}-\\partial_iF_{0j}+\\partial_jF_{0i}=0$. Since $\\partial_sA_i=F_{0i}$, this gives $\\partial_sB_{ij}=0$. Therefore $B_{ij}=0$ persists from its initial value. Together with the initial equality $F_{ij}=\\partial_iA_j-\\partial_jA_i$ and $F_{0i}=\\partial_sA_i-\\partial_iA_0=\\partial_sA_i$, all components satisfy $F=dA$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L5",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "With $\\pi=D\\varphi$, the reduced divergence equation is the actual scalar equation $E_\\varphi=0$. Substituting it into [L3] gives $\\nabla_aj^a=0$ and $\\nabla_a(T^\\varphi)^a{}_b=F_{ba}j^a$. To fix the sourced form sign, put $v^b:=\\nabla_aF^{ab}$. Since $\\nabla$ preserves the metric and oriented volume, it preserves the Hodge star, and torsion-freeness lets $d\\star_gF$ be the antisymmetrized covariant derivative of $\\star_gF$. In a positively oriented orthonormal frame, expanding the Hodge components in [L7] gives $(d\\star_gF)_{123}=-v^0$ and $(d\\star_gF)_{0ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}v^k$; the corresponding components of $\\iota_v\\mathrm{vol}_g$ are $v^0$ and $-\\epsilon_{ijk}v^k$. These are all four independent components of the three-forms, so $d\\star_gF=-\\iota_v\\mathrm{vol}_g$. Since $v=-\\mu_0j$ by [L1], this is exactly $d\\star_gF=\\mu_0\\iota_j\\mathrm{vol}_g$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L3",
            "L7",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Put $U:=dF$ and $W:=d\\star_gF-\\mu_0\\iota_j\\mathrm{vol}_g$. On $\\Sigma$, $F_\\Sigma=\\iota_b\\mathrm{vol}_h$, so $d_\\Sigma F_\\Sigma=(D_i b^i)\\mathrm{vol}_h$ because $D\\mathrm{vol}_h=0$. The Hodge formula in [L7] gives $(\\star_gF)_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}e_k$ in the stated normal frame, hence $d_\\Sigma(\\star_gF)=(D_i e^i)\\mathrm{vol}_h$; also $(\\iota_j\\mathrm{vol}_g)|_\\Sigma=j^0\\mathrm{vol}_h$. Thus the two Gauss constraints are exactly $U|_\\Sigma=W|_\\Sigma=0$. The evolution equations in the Statement give $\\iota_TU=\\iota_TW=0$. Decompose any three-form $Q$ on the product region as $Q=ds\\wedge q_2(s)+q_3(s)$ with spatial forms $q_2,q_3$; then $\\iota_TQ=q_2$, so $Q=q_3$. Since $dQ=0$, its product-coordinate exterior derivative is $ds\\wedge\\partial_sq_3+d_\\Sigma q_3=0$; the second term vanishes because it is a spatial four-form on the three-dimensional slice. Hence $\\partial_sq_3=0$. Apply this first to $U$, for which $dU=d^2F=0$, and then to $W$, for which $dW=-\\mu_0(\\nabla_aj^a)\\mathrm{vol}_g=0$ by [L8] and step 2.2. Their initial spatial forms are zero, so $U=W=0$ on the local product region. At the stated Sobolev regularity these exterior-form identities hold distributionally, and the $H^s$, $s\\ge5$, solution has continuous spatial representatives and initial traces, so the same zero-initial-value argument applies. This proves the full sourced Maxwell equations.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L5",
            "L7",
            "L8",
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Combining the on-shell scalar identity from step 2.2 with [L4] gives $\\nabla_aT^{a}{}_b=F_{ba}j^a-F_{ba}j^a=0$, equivalently $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ because $T$ is symmetric. This conservation holds before the Einstein equation is recovered.",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "L3",
            "L4",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Let $E_{ab}:=G_{ab}+\\Lambda g_{ab}-\\kappa T_{ab}$. By [L9]–[L10], $E_{ab}=\\tfrac12(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a-g_{ab}\\nabla_cH^c)$. Since $H|_\\Sigma=0$, its tangential covariant derivatives vanish there. In an orthonormal frame $(n,e_i)$, $\\nabla_cH^c=-\\nabla_nH_n$ on $\\Sigma$, so $E(n,n)=\\tfrac12\\nabla_nH_n$ and $E(n,e_i)=\\tfrac12\\nabla_nH_i$. The normal Einstein projections in [L11] and the assumed initial constraints set both residual components to zero. Therefore $H$ and its normal derivative vanish on $\\Sigma$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L9",
            "L10",
            "L11",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "step": "6.1",
          "claim": "The homogeneous wave equation in [L10] is a linear wave system with smooth lower-order curvature coefficients. In a smaller spacelike coordinate cylinder, write its principal part as $g^{00}\\partial_s^2H_b+2g^{0i}\\partial_s\\partial_iH_b+g^{ij}\\partial_i\\partial_jH_b$ and introduce $v_b=\\partial_sH_b$ and $w_{bi}=\\partial_iH_b$. The compatibility equations are $\\partial_sw_{bi}-\\partial_iv_b=0$; substituting them into the wave equation gives the symmetric matrices of a scalar wave reduction, with time matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(-g^{00},(g^{ij}))$ repeated over $b$. Thus the first-order state $Y=(H,v,w)$ has a positive time matrix and symmetric spatial matrices, while connection and curvature terms are bounded zero-order coefficients on a compact cylinder. Choose a ball shrinking at speed $\\sigma$ greater than $\\sup_{|\\xi|=1}\\|\\xi_iA^i\\|/\\lambda_{\\min}(A^0)$. For $E(s)=\\int_{B_{r-\\sigma s}}Y^{\\mathsf T}A^0Y\\,dx$, multiplying the system by $2Y^{\\mathsf T}$ and using symmetry gives a divergence identity whose interior terms are bounded by $C E(s)$; the side flux is nonnegative because $\\sigma A^0+\\nu_iA^i$ is positive definite. The finite-domain divergence theorem [L12] therefore gives $E'(s)\\le C E(s)$. The initial data from step 5.1 give $Y=0$ and $E(0)=0$, so Gronwall [L12] yields $E(s)=0$; reverse time for $s<0$. Hence $H=0$ near every initial point, and the reduced equation has $E_{ab}=0$, which is the full Einstein equation. For the stated Sobolev regularity, [L13] supplies the classical derivatives in these identities.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L9",
            "L10",
            "L12",
            "L13",
            "A2",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
