    "thm-emg-local-classical-charged-scalar-development": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-emg-trivial-potential-charged-scalar-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The associated **classical charged-scalar Einstein–Maxwell system** is",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For finite $m\\ge1$, open $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, compact $K\\subseteq O$, $R\\ge0$, and integer $s\\ge5$, define the bounded admissible initial-state class",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Coefficients.** A **periodic symmetric hyperbolic coefficient family** on\n$O$ consists of smooth maps\n$$A^\\mu:I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O\\to\\operatorname{Sym}_m(\\mathbb R)\\quad(\\mu=0,1,2,3),\\qquad B:I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O\\to\\mathbb R^m,$$\nsuch that $A^0(t,x,u)$ is positive definite for every\n$(t,x,u)\\in I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$; here\n$\\operatorname{Sym}_m(\\mathbb R)$ is the real vector space of symmetric\n$m\\times m$ matrices, identified with symmetric endomorphisms of $\\mathbb R^m$\nthrough the standard coordinate inner product, and positive definiteness means\n$z^TA^0(t,x,u)z>0$ for every nonzero $z\\in\\mathbb R^m$\n([[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L3",
          "source": "thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer, let $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $A^\\mu,B$ ($\\mu=0,1,2,3$) be a periodic symmetric-hyperbolic coefficient family on $I\\times\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi}\\times O$ in the exact sense of [[def-gr-periodic-symmetric-hyperbolic-system]]: the coefficient maps are smooth, each $A^\\mu$ is a real symmetric matrix, and $A^0$ is positive definite. Let $K\\subseteq\\operatorname{int}K'\\subseteq O$ be compact and let $R\\ge0$. Then there are $T>0$ and $C<\\infty$, uniform over all $u_0\\in\\mathcal S_s(K,R)$ of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]], such that the system",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L4",
          "source": "lem-emg-fixed-smooth-background-harmonic-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then each entry of this first metric jet has the regularity required by the first-order state class: the metric values are $H^{s+1}$ and the prescribed first derivatives are $H^s$. The harmonic-gauge vector $H^a=g^{bc}(\\Gamma(g)^a{}_{bc}-\\Gamma(\\bar g)^a{}_{bc})$ of [[def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations]] vanishes on the initial slice. The background $\\gamma$ is required to be smooth; choosing $\\gamma=h$ is not asserted to be valid for finite-regularity $h$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "lem-emg-fieldstrength-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The six evolution equations for $(e,b)$ have principal part\n$$n(e)-\\operatorname{curl}_h b=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b),\\qquad n(b)+\\operatorname{curl}_h e=\\text{terms with no derivatives of }(e,b).$$\nFor a spatial covector $\\xi$, let $C(\\xi)v:=\\xi^\\sharp\\times v$ in the oriented orthonormal frame. Set $\\theta^0:=\\alpha ds=-g(n,\\cdot)$ and decompose any spacetime covector as $\\zeta=\\zeta(n)\\theta^0+\\zeta_\\perp$, where $\\zeta_\\perp(n)=0$ and $|\\zeta_\\perp|_h$ is its spatial norm. The spatial principal block is the linear map on $\\mathbb R^3\\oplus\\mathbb R^3$ defined by $S(\\xi)(u,v)=(-C(\\xi)v,C(\\xi)u)$. It is real symmetric, has eigenvalues $0,0,|\\xi|,|\\xi|,-|\\xi|,-|\\xi|$, and satisfies $\\|S(\\xi)\\|=|\\xi|$. Therefore the principal symbol $P(\\zeta)=\\zeta(n)I_6+S(\\zeta_\\perp)$ is positive definite for every covector with $\\zeta(n)>|\\zeta_\\perp|_h$. In the adapted coordinates its time matrix is $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$, and every coordinate spatial matrix is symmetric. Derivatives of the frame and metric enter only as smooth lower-order coefficients depending on $(g,g^{-1},\\partial g,e,b)$; in a first-order metric state they are zero-order terms. The remaining two Maxwell equations are the constraints $\\operatorname{div}_h e=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}_h b=0$.\n\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L5",
          "source": "lem-emg-coupled-reduced-einstein-evolution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $s\\ge5$ be an integer and let $U=(g_{ab},p_{ab},q_{abi},e_i,b_i)$ be the finite-dimensional first-order state on the $2\\pi$-periodic three-torus, with $p_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$ and $q_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$ for a fixed smooth background connection $\\bar\\nabla$. The component Sobolev spaces $H^s$ are those of [[def-emg-periodic-high-sobolev-state]]. Assume an open admissible state set $O$ on which $g$ is Lorentzian, $g^{00}<0$, the inverse spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite, the lapse is positive, and the chosen state variables and coefficients are smooth. Let $K\\Subset\\operatorname{int}K'\\Subset O$ be compact sets and $R\\ge0$. Assume the draft local symmetric-hyperbolic supplier [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]] with its stated integer-$s$, coefficient, compact-state, choice and norm hypotheses. Then the harmonic-coordinate reduced Einstein equation with source $T_{ab}=\\mu_0^{-1}(F_{ac}F_b{}^c-\\tfrac14g_{ab}F_{cd}F^{cd})$, coupled to the six source-free Maxwell field-strength evolution equations, is a smooth quasilinear symmetric-hyperbolic system. For every initial state $U_0\\in H^s$ taking values in $K$ and satisfying $\\|U_0\\|_{H^s}\\le R$, its reduced system has a local solution with the regularity and compact-state bounds of [[thm-emg-symmetric-hyperbolic-research-supplier]]. No Einstein or Maxwell constraint is assumed in this reduced-system statement.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L6",
          "source": "lem-emg-temporal-scalar-positive-symmetric-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "where $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ and $D_a\\pi^a:=g^{ab}D_a\\pi_b$. The temporal-gauge scalar first-order system is",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L7",
          "source": "lem-emg-charged-scalar-subsidiary-chain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then on a neighborhood of every initial point:",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L8",
          "source": "def-gr-harmonic-gauge-and-reduced-equations",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(a) Gauge vector.** The **harmonic-gauge vector field** of the pair\n$(g,\\bar g)$ is the smooth vector field\n$$H^a:=g^{bc}\\bigl(\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\\bar\\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\\bigr) .$$\nThe difference of the connection coefficients is a $(1,2)$-tensor, so $H$ is a\nwell-defined smooth vector field independent of the chart used to express the\nsymbols ([[def-gr-tensor-fields-and-components]]).\n\n**(b) Reduced Einstein equation.** The **reduced Einstein equation** for the\npair $(g,T)$ with respect to $\\bar g$ is the second-order quasilinear system\n$$\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}-\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\kappa\\bigl(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12Tg_{ab}\\bigr)+\\Lambda g_{ab},$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{Ric}_{ab}$ is the Ricci tensor of $g$\n([[def-gr-curvature-tensor-and-derived-tensors]]),\n$H_b=g_{bc}H^c$ and $\\nabla_{(a}H_{b)}=\\tfrac12(\\nabla_aH_b+\\nabla_bH_a)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L8",
          "source": "lem-gr-reduced-einstein-principal-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b) [first-order reduction]** Fix constants $\\kappa>0$, $\\Lambda\\in\\mathbb R$ and a prescribed smooth symmetric covariant $2$-tensor field $T$ on the chart domain. Fix a chart $(x^0,x^1,x^2,x^3)$ with\n$x^0=t$. Introduce the metric variables\n$$u:=\\bigl(g_{ab},\\ p_{ab}:=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab},\\ q_{abi}:=\\bar\\nabla_ig_{ab}\\bigr)$$\n(symmetry in $a,b$ understood) and the relation variables $\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}-p_{ab}=0$ and $\\bar\\nabla_0q_{abi}-\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}=\\mathcal C_{abi}$, where $\\mathcal C_{abi}=-\\bar R^c{}_{a0i}g_{cb}-\\bar R^c{}_{b0i}g_{ac}$. Second covariant derivatives include their derivative-slot connection. These known background-curvature terms are zeroth order in the first-order state and vanish for the flat coordinate background. Then the reduced Einstein equation can be solved for the second time derivatives in the form\n$$\\bar\\nabla_0p_{ab}=-(g^{00})^{-1}\\Bigl[2g^{0i}\\bar\\nabla_ip_{ab}+g^{ij}\\bar\\nabla_iq_{abj}+R_{ab}(x,u,T;\\kappa,\\Lambda,\\bar g)\\Bigr],$$\nwhere the source is smooth in the first-order metric state and the prescribed matter data, with no derivatives of $T$, and includes $2\\kappa(T_{ab}-\\tfrac12(g^{cd}T_{cd})g_{ab})+2\\Lambda g_{ab}$. The resulting first-order system for $u$ is symmetric hyperbolic on every\nregion where $g^{00}<0$ and the spatial block $(g^{ij})$ is positive definite:\nthe coefficient of $\\bar\\nabla_0u$ is the block diagonal\n$\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,g^{ij}I)$ (on the three slots\n$g,p,q$), which is positive definite there, and the coefficients of the spatial\nderivatives are symmetric. More generally, in a coupled reduction one may substitute a specified smooth algebraic stress map $T_{ab}=\\mathcal T_{ab}(x,u,z)$, symmetric in $a,b$, where $z$ is a specified additional first-order matter state ranging in an open Euclidean set. This keeps the same metric principal matrices; symmetric hyperbolicity of the combined metric-matter system requires a separate check of the matter equations.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L9",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice]]). Let integers $s\\ge m\\ge0$ satisfy $s-m>3/2$, and\nlet $u\\in H^s(\\mathbb T^3_{2\\pi};\\mathbb R^N)$ with the objects and notation of\n[[def-gr-periodic-torus-and-sobolev-spaces]]. Then:",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L9",
          "source": "lem-gr-periodic-sobolev-algebra",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(b)** the pointwise product $uv$ of the continuous representatives lies in\n$H^k$ and\n$$\\|uv\\|_{H^k}\\le C_k\\,\\|u\\|_{H^k}\\,\\|v\\|_{H^k};$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L10",
          "source": "thm-gr-constraint-equations-in-normal-frame",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(c) Normal projections.** Define $(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(Y)=h^{ij}(D_{e_i}K)(e_j,Y)$, $\\tau=\\operatorname{tr}_hK=h^{ij}K_{ij}$ and $|K|_h^2=h^{ik}h^{jl}K_{ij}K_{kl}$. Then\n$$G(n,n)=\\tfrac12(S_h+\\tau^2-|K|_h^2),\\qquad G(n,Y)=(\\operatorname{div}_hK)(Y)-D_Y\\tau.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L11",
          "source": "thm-gr-local-geometric-development",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then the data have a smooth time-oriented local development $(g,\\Psi)$ on an open variable-width neighborhood of $\\{0\\}\\times\\Sigma$ in the literal manifold $\\mathbb R\\times\\Sigma$, with induced data $(h,K,d)$, solving\n$$G(g)+\\Lambda g=\\kappa\\mathcal T(g,\\Psi),\\qquad\\mathcal E(g,\\Psi)=0.$$\nAfter restriction to a smaller collar, $\\Sigma$ lies in the interior of its intrinsic domain of dependence. The interior of that domain is a globally hyperbolic neighborhood with $\\Sigma$ Cauchy. Any two developments of this same model and these complete data are related near the entire initial surface by a data-fixing time-orientation-preserving isometry matching all matter fields. There is no uniform noncompact time width or global-in-time conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "4.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The prescribed normal/tangential two-form components determine the unique real two-form on $\\mathbb R n\\oplus T\\Sigma$: $F(n,\\mathfrak e_i)=-e_i$ and $F(\\mathfrak e_i,\\mathfrak e_j)=\\epsilon_{ijk}b_k$. Exactness $F_\\Sigma=d_\\Sigma A_\\Sigma$ and $\\pi_i=D_i\\varphi_0$, together with $\\pi_0=\\pi_n$ in the initial temporal gauge, make all field and potential entries compatible. The jet in [L4] makes the harmonic vector initially zero. After the fixed invertible rescalings, every component is real and belongs to the stipulated $H^s$ class; the compact range lies in $K_0\\Subset K_1\\Subset O$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "L4",
            "A1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "On $O$, the metric block has the positive time matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(I,-g^{00}I,(g^{ij})I)$ from [L5] and [L8]; the total stress is algebraic in $(g,F,\\varphi,\\pi)$, so the scalar contribution changes only zero-order terms. The Maxwell block has time matrix $\\alpha^{-1}I_6$ and symmetric spatial matrices by [L5]; the sourced current is algebraic in $(g,\\varphi,\\pi)$ and hence is lower order. The charged scalar block has time matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(-g^{00},(g^{ij}))$ on each real and imaginary part by [L6]. The $\\varphi$ and $A_i$ transport equations add identity time blocks and zero spatial blocks. These blocks have no off-diagonal derivative couplings, so their direct sum is symmetric with positive time matrix. All coefficient maps are smooth on $O$ by smooth inversion of $g$, smoothness of $V$, and the stipulated open range.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L2",
            "L5",
            "L6",
            "L8",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Apply [L3] to this verified coefficient family, the state $u_0\\in\\mathcal S_s(K_0,R)$, and compact sets $K_0\\Subset K_1\\Subset O$. Conditional on the current draft solver statement, this gives the common interval, values in $K_1$, $L^\\infty H^s\\cap C H^{s-1}$ state regularity, uniqueness and stability, and smooth persistence for smooth initial data. It asserts only a solution to the reduced first-order system at this step.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L1",
            "L2",
            "L3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "The initial metric, scalar, potential, and Maxwell compatibilities and all Gauss and Einstein constraints are exactly those of [L7]. Applying that conditional subsidiary result recovers $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$, $q_i=\\bar\\nabla_i g$, $\\pi=D\\varphi$, $F=dA$, both sourced Maxwell equations, total-stress conservation before Einstein recovery, and $H=0$. Therefore the reduced solution is a full solution of the mathematical system in [F1].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L4",
            "L7",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "Since $q_i=\\partial_i g$ up to the fixed smooth background connection, the periodic Fourier norm identity gives $\\|g\\|_{H^{s+1}}\\asymp\\|g\\|_{H^s}+\\sum_i\\|\\partial_i g\\|_{H^s}$; similarly $p=\\bar\\nabla_0g$ gives the stated time-derivative regularity. From $\\pi_i=D_i\\varphi$, one has $\\partial_i\\varphi=\\pi_i+i\\lambda A_i\\varphi$. The $H^s$ algebra estimate in [L9] makes $A_i\\varphi\\in L^\\infty H^s$, and the same estimate in $C H^{s-1}$ gives continuity of the spatial derivatives. The Fourier norm identity $\\|f\\|_{H^{r+1}}\\asymp\\|f\\|_{H^r}+\\sum_i\\|\\partial_i f\\|_{H^r}$ then gives $\\varphi\\in L^\\infty H^{s+1}\\cap C H^s$. The field-strength, potential, and $\\pi$ regularities are the corresponding components of the reduced state. The embedding in [L9] supplies the classical derivatives needed by the equations at $s\\ge5$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "L7",
            "L9",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "step": "4.2",
          "claim": "For general smooth $\\Sigma$, regard $\\Psi=(A,\\operatorname{Re}\\varphi,\\operatorname{Im}\\varphi)$ as the fixed covariant matter fields; $F=dA$, $\\pi=D\\varphi$, $j$, and $T$ are the fixed equations and tensorial expressions of [F1]. On each relatively compact initial chart choose a flat comparison metric $\\bar g=-(dx^0)^2+\\delta_{ij}dx^i dx^j$, set $g_{ij}=h_{ij}$, $g_{00}=-1$, $g_{0i}=0$, $\\partial_0g_{ij}=2K_{ij}$, $\\partial_0g_{00}=-2\\tau$, and $\\partial_0g_{0i}=h_{ij}h^{k\\ell}\\Gamma(h)^j{}_{k\\ell}$. The Christoffel formula gives $H^0=\\tfrac12\\partial_0g_{00}+\\tau=0$ and $H^i=-h^{ij}\\partial_0g_{0j}+h^{k\\ell}\\Gamma(h)^i{}_{k\\ell}=0$ initially. Set $q_{abi}=\\bar\\nabla_i g_{ab}$, $p_{ab}=\\bar\\nabla_0g_{ab}$, reconstruct $F$ from $(e,b)$, and use $\\pi_i=D_i\\varphi_0$, $\\pi_0=\\pi_n$, and $A_0=0$. Thus the complete data and all initial auxiliary constraints are specified; $A(n)=0$ fixes the initial temporal gauge. The equations and stress are covariant under local diffeomorphisms because $d$, the metric connection, contractions, and the oriented volume form commute with pullback. Steps 1.2 and 3.1 give the exact smooth symmetric-hyperbolic reduction, propagation of every auxiliary constraint, recovery of the matter equations, and $\\nabla^aT_{ab}=0$ before Einstein recovery. The initial constraints are precisely the normal projections in [L10]. These are the explicit reduction, initialization, conservation, and constraint hypotheses of [L11], so its conditional theorem gives a local development near each initial point.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L5",
            "L6",
            "L7",
            "L8",
            "L10",
            "L11",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 3.1",
            "A1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "Apply the data-fixing geometric uniqueness clause of [L11] to the fixed covariant model and complete data; the periodic torus is included as a smooth three-manifold without boundary. After pulling one development back by the resulting isometry, if an internal gauge change is needed to compare the chosen representatives and its temporal component is $A'_0$, define on the product collar $\\chi(s,x):=-\\int_0^s A'_0(\\sigma,x)\\,d\\sigma$. This is smooth, vanishes on $\\Sigma$, and makes $A'_0+\\partial_s\\chi=0$. Under $A'\\mapsto A'+d\\chi$ and $\\varphi'\\mapsto e^{i\\lambda\\chi}\\varphi'$, direct differentiation gives $D'\\varphi'\\mapsto e^{i\\lambda\\chi}D'\\varphi'$ and $F'=dA'$ is unchanged; hence the current, both stress tensors, and all equations are invariant. Since $\\chi|_\\Sigma=0$, the tangential initial potential, scalar, and covariant normal derivative are unchanged. Thus [L11]'s matching of the complete data yields the stated local isometry and, when needed, the displayed global-potential gauge equivalence; no periodic solver uniqueness is being applied to nonperiodic data.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "L11",
            "step 4.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
