          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-emg-local-classical-charged-scalar-development",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "More generally, let $\\Sigma$ be any smooth oriented three-manifold without boundary. Prescribe smooth $h$ positive definite, symmetric $K$, spatial vector fields $e,b$, a global one-form $A_\\Sigma$, a complex scalar $\\varphi_0$, and its independent normal derivative $\\pi_n$. Define $F_\\Sigma$ by the same $h,e,b$ convention in local positively oriented orthonormal frames, without requiring a global frame. Require $F_\\Sigma=d_\\Sigma A_\\Sigma$, $\\pi_i=D_i\\varphi_0$, $A(n)=0$, and the sourced Maxwell and total-stress Einstein constraints displayed above at every point of $\\Sigma$. Restrict to neighborhoods where $|\\varphi|^2\\in\\mathcal U$. Then the data have a smooth local development near each initial point. Any two developments of this same fixed model and complete data are related near the initial surface by a data-fixing, time-orientation-preserving isometry and a global-potential gauge transformation",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-emg-local-classical-charged-scalar-development",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "More generally, let $\\Sigma$ be any smooth oriented three-manifold without boundary. Prescribe smooth $h$ positive definite, symmetric $K$, spatial vector fields $e,b$, a global one-form $A_\\Sigma$, a complex scalar $\\varphi_0$, and its independent normal derivative $\\pi_n$. Define $F_\\Sigma$ by the same $h,e,b$ convention in local positively oriented orthonormal frames, without requiring a global frame. Require $F_\\Sigma=d_\\Sigma A_\\Sigma$, $\\pi_i=D_i\\varphi_0$, $A(n)=0$, and the sourced Maxwell and total-stress Einstein constraints displayed above at every point of $\\Sigma$. Restrict to neighborhoods where $|\\varphi|^2\\in\\mathcal U$. Then the data have a smooth local development near each initial point. Any two developments of this same fixed model and complete data are related near the initial surface by a data-fixing, time-orientation-preserving isometry and a global-potential gauge transformation",
          "uses": [
